{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789481856,"nonce":"qfwbUFcrr1U9kF1WSc33RB+56d8=","title":"The collector marks a live set that is still being built, several times over","message":"A program that builds a table pays a collection at the floor and then at each growth step of the budget, and each of those marks everything and frees nothing. Two defects in the policy and a slow marker made it worse than it need be:\n\n1. The growth test counted large blocks: one dead 4 MB list buffer (a superseded storage block) read as a productive collection on bench_tokenize and reset the growth to 1, so the next collection came after as much again and marked 27 MB for nothing. Now only the slabs count: a large block is freed without being marked, and one dead buffer says nothing about the rest of the heap.\n\n2. The growth climbed 1, 2, 4 across collections that each found nothing. Now a collection that finds little in the slabs sets it to the cap at once; one that finds plenty resets it to 1.\n\n3. The marker paid two hash lookups and an integer division per word landing in a slab, searched the large-block tree for every word that landed in no slab (words pointing at the shared static boxes did, 700 k of them on bench_records), and re-looked-up every repeated pointer (a row's four key boxes, a box's pointer to its own payload). Now: a paged table of slab bits with the last page to hand, a multiply by a reciprocal, a set of the spans large blocks cover, a skip of words that point back into the block being scanned, and a four-entry cache of pointers already found marked (a first pointer to a block is not cached, so a block pointed to once does not evict the shared ones; with it cached the cache hit 9 times in 2.4 M lookups).\n\nMeasured on the M1 Pro, exec ms with collections in brackets: bench_pipeline 78 [3 + 6 + 14] to 56 [3]; bench_tokenize 120 [9 + 16 + 16] to 100 [8 + 14]; bench_records 200 [7 + 21] to 194 [5 + 26], its second collection now at four times the live set, marking 75 MB in 26 ms where it took 44 before the marker work. Marking is about 1.5 ns per word scanned plus 8 ns per block marked; the rest of a building program's cost is the words that are not pointers (a box's tag, size, dropper, display), which only a typed scan could skip.\n\n`ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB` and `ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=1` as before.\n\nNot done: a generational or Immix-style nursery, which is what makes a building program cheap to collect (b94b4cb).","files":null}]}